Real-World Example

Suppose a banking app needs a new feature: UPI QR Payments.

Agile
Product Owner creates a user story.
Team plans it in Sprint Planning.
Developers implement it.
QA tests it.
Sprint review is conducted.
DevOps
Developer pushes code to GitHub.
GitHub Actions builds the application.
SonarQube checks code quality.
Trivy scans for vulnerabilities.
Docker image is pushed to ACR.
Helm deploys it to AKS.
Azure Monitor tracks performance.
Alerts are generated if issues occur.

Agile delivers the feature.

DevOps ensures the feature reaches users safely and reliably.

Sprint vs Release

This is another common interview topic.

Sprint	Release
Time-boxed development cycle	Software made available to users
Usually 1–4 weeks	Can happen anytime
Managed by Agile	Managed through DevOps/Release Management

A sprint does not always end with a production release. Multiple sprints may be combined into a single release, or continuous delivery may allow releases after every completed feature.

